<h2>The statutory design</h2>
<p>Section 23 sits at the end of the Act&rsquo;s relief chapter and energises everything before it. Sub-section (1) is the general interim power: in any proceeding before him under the Act, the Magistrate may pass such interim order as he deems just and proper. Sub-section (2) is the emergency power: where the Magistrate is satisfied that the application prima facie discloses that the respondent is committing, has committed, or is likely to commit an act of domestic violence, he may grant an ex parte order on the basis of the aggrieved person&rsquo;s affidavit, in respect of the reliefs under Sections 18, 19, 20, 21 and 22.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 18 — protection.</strong> Prohibitions on violence, contact, entering the workplace or school, alienating assets, intimidation.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 19 — residence.</strong> Restraint from dispossession, removal from the shared household in defined cases, alternative accommodation.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 20 — money.</strong> Loss of earnings, medical expenses, maintenance for the woman and children.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Sections 21 and 22 — custody and compensation.</strong> Temporary custody arrangements and compensation for injuries, including mental torture and emotional distress.</div>
</div>

<h2>The threshold: prima facie disclosure, not proof</h2>
<p>The ex parte stage asks whether the application and affidavit disclose domestic violence, not whether they prove it. That is a deliberately low bar — the provision exists because insisting on notice and reply can defeat the relief itself. But the bar is not nothing. The satisfaction must be the Magistrate&rsquo;s own, formed on the affidavit and the material accompanying the application; orders that simply reproduce the prayer clause invite appellate correction. And the breadth of relief should be proportionate to the urgency shown: stopping dispossession tonight is a different order from directing substantial monthly payments before the respondent&rsquo;s income is even asserted on oath.</p>

<h2>The respondent&rsquo;s playbook</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appear immediately.</strong> An ex parte order is interim by definition; the fastest correction is a prompt appearance with a reply affidavit and documents — income proof, ownership papers, prior proceedings.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Seek modification.</strong> The Magistrate who passed the order can vary or vacate it; Section 25(2) additionally permits alteration on a change of circumstances at any time.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appeal where needed.</strong> Section 29 provides an appeal to the Court of Session within thirty days of service — the route for orders infected by jurisdictional or threshold defects.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Comply meanwhile.</strong> Until modified or set aside, the order binds. Breach of a protection order is independently punishable under Section 31.</div>
</div>

<h2>Practice notes for the applicant&rsquo;s side</h2>
<p>The quality of the affidavit decides the ex parte stage. Dates, specific incidents, medical or police records where they exist, and a precise description of the shared household convert a plea into an order. Overreach is counterproductive: a tightly drawn protection-and-residence order obtained ex parte, followed by a contested hearing on monetary relief with an assets affidavit, survives appeal far better than an omnibus order granting everything on day one. Where dispossession is threatened, the residence order should identify the premises and the specific restraint sought, so that enforcement through the protection officer and police under the Act&rsquo;s machinery is mechanical rather than contestable.</p>
<div class="note">This explainer states the general statutory scheme for information. Interim relief in any actual case turns on the pleadings, the affidavit and the Magistrate&rsquo;s assessment of urgency.</div>
